16
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,000
strategy Nash equilibria and one mixed strategy Nash equilibria.

17
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,000
But sometimes there will be questions that ask you about the real world.

18
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:12,000
And if the question asks you about the real world, is it okay to say assume Bertrand

19
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:16,000
or Assume Chrono Collusion

20
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:20,000
or assume anything. If it's a question about the real world,

21
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:24,000
you need to actually argue. So it's good to use a model from class, but you want

22
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:28,000
to begin by arguing the model's relevance.

23
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:32,000
Or at least close. Say, this model may not be perfect, but it's a reasonable

24
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:36,000
approximation in this particular case we're talking about.

25
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:40,000
Similarly, if there's a question like, is it a good model?

26
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:44,000
In explaining the real world. You don't want to just give some circular thing

27
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:48,000
saying, of course it's a good model because it's internally consistent.

28
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:52,000
Instead, if the question asks you to assess the empirical relevance of the model,

29
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,000
you need to understand the model well enough to see discrepancies and then

30
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:00,000
talk about them.

